#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 1440.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130216} :activity/id #uuid "c10a40bf-815b-59d3-8b54-688b2275327e" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048736 :topic/id #uuid "b9fc7121-4ed8-5254-ad37-ccaadac27c0e" :topic/math-academy-id 2028} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130216 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130459 :tutorial/content "Consider a plane lamina that occupies the region $D \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}^2$ of mass $M$ where the center of mass is at $\\left(\\bar{x},\\bar{y}\\right).$\n\n![](images/4c/4c2c7e3525dfe8aea3cce69a7332ccf5ca82ddfe37657071ddef35650bcea383.png)\n\nWe know that the lamina has some moment of inertia $I_M$ about the line $l_M$ that passes through the center of mass and is perpendicular to the lamina.\n\n![](images/96/9638e9d47ef3c9b2fab47f39cf70e2ba07863c6e949755c8378e5737caa0a350.png)\n\nNow, if $l$ is a line parallel to $l_M,$ then the lamina also has moment of inertia about $l,$ say $I_l.$\n\n![](images/06/06819ca45bd176ff9d36b07c73574bc9f2911a7b0b96b5b916ba76f8606d2f60.png)\n\nGiven the value of $I_M,$ we can find $I_l$ using the **parallel axis theorem**, which states that\n\n$$\nI_l = I_M + d^2M,\n$$\n\nwhere $d$ is the distance between the lines.\n\nThe term $d^2M$ is the moment of inertia about $l$ of the lamina when its entire mass is concentrated at the center of mass.\n\n![](images/fb/fbab9fab43dd45fd80008704f406f94864cac57d636320d250dca86ff465f196.png)\n\nSo, our interpretation of the parallel axis theorem is the following:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\text{moment of inertia of }D \\ \\text{about a parallel line }l}_{I_l}\n\\:=\\:\n\\underbrace{\\text{moment of inertia of }D \\ \\text{about the center of mass}}_{I_M}\n\\:+\\:\n\\underbrace{\\text{moment of inertia of point mass }M \\ \\text{about the parallel line } l}_{d^2M}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $d^2M$ is always positive, we have $I_M \\leq I_l$ for any line $l$ perpendicular to the lamina. In other words, the moment of inertia about the line through its center of mass is the *smallest* moment of inertia about any line in that direction." :tutorial/id #uuid "b1dda94b-0ea5-5ff2-a3d5-a406dfc9b95d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7332 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "aae6c762-bad6-59ad-bbac-4dd3947ca34e" :step/math-academy-id 43340 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130217}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130217 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129273 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128850 :question/id #uuid "915a7c2d-3797-5dfa-8d68-85e2546a23d6" :question/math-academy-id "e-11778"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "35de55bc-23b5-574c-9fab-33c4bca720fe" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048585}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129545} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129546} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235556} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284430} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284431} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284432} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284433} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284434} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431765}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Moment of Inertia About a Perpendicular Axis"} :step/id #uuid "a371a864-c197-5aa0-8d21-3cead8839e82" :step/math-academy-id 43341 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130262}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130218 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129274 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128851 :question/id #uuid "153b828d-545a-58fb-bc81-0d7311726a87" :question/math-academy-id "e-11779"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "850f196a-9607-56c2-b0a7-a00d4d5a4741" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129549} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129693} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284428} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284429} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326694} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372314} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389504} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431762} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431764} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500372}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Moment of Inertia About a Perpendicular Axis Using Polar Coordinates"} :step/id #uuid "2f9433b3-3716-5cab-88dc-0c8106c423cf" :step/math-academy-id 43342 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130249}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130249 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129289 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128864 :question/id #uuid "72ccaeab-ad76-50c7-8b51-7736c5270521" :question/math-academy-id "e-12493"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c5c774e2-7c3f-56a9-99b6-96aca1cd4f49" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129550} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129670} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284440} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284441} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284446} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372258} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372265} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372279} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431761} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500327} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500335}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Distance Between Two Parallel Axes"} :step/id #uuid "942eea0b-0401-5ec3-980b-a17bb6c3cdaa" :step/math-academy-id 44997} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130250 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129290 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128865 :question/id #uuid "131948d9-d533-5316-bcd3-b7859244892e" :question/math-academy-id "e-12494"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "21f203c5-4866-5077-9ef9-0d2122581c4f" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129547} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129548} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284443} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284445} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372272} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372286} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389490} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389497} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431763} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431766}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Moment of Inertia of a Rectangular Plate About a Perpendicular Axis"} :step/id #uuid "e5790220-0fbc-5caf-b3ee-9039c4289a67" :step/math-academy-id 45003 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130218}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130262 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130480 :tutorial/content "In a previous lesson, we saw that the moment of inertia about an axis $l$ of a plane lamina occupying the region $D$ with mass density function $\\lambda(x,y)$ is given by\n\n$$\nI_l = \\iint\\limits_D [r(x,y)]^2\\,\\lambda(x,y)  \\:\\textrm{d}A.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia of $D$ about the line through the center of mass $\\left(\\bar{x}, \\bar{y}\\right)$ and perpendicular to the lamina is given by\n\n$$\nI_M = \\iint\\limits_D \\left((x-\\bar{x})^2+(y-\\bar{y})^2\\right) \\lambda(x,y) \\:\\textrm{d}A.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "6a08f490-ccca-5a93-adb8-dbf845e4d4cc" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7849 :tutorial/title "A Formula for the Moment of Inertia About the Line Through the Center of Mass"} :step/id #uuid "f23fbe74-8ade-5fcf-9513-af6482f642b2" :step/math-academy-id 45317 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130250}}] :activity/title "The Parallel Axis Theorem" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186129370}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046660} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500404} :activity/id #uuid "a6c4c785-a86b-556d-b318-805973a4f043" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048736 :topic/id #uuid "b9fc7121-4ed8-5254-ad37-ccaadac27c0e" :topic/math-academy-id 2028} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500385 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500327 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25762675-e43f-5321-928d-4a741bf441a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1\\,\\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499738} {:answer/id #uuid "445175a5-5a24-523c-b84c-ed125868ae4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}\\,\\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499784} {:answer/id #uuid "4b5b46eb-59d7-5a40-a5bd-d482010a3ba7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}\\,\\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499798} {:answer/id #uuid "5aebe913-e02e-5668-9447-061656dd07be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{4}\\,\\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499829} {:answer/id #uuid "fb14c01b-fcc2-56c9-94a5-1c158a4817f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{9}\\,\\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500170}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499798} :answer-field/id #uuid "a6b72617-42bc-5f67-b04d-32f60467b95a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500250}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "92c86cbd-ae23-56f6-b895-3b862f7ae3ec" :question/math-academy-id "q-163218" :question/problem "A rectangular plate occupies the region\n$D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 3,\\,0\\le y\\le 3\\}$\nand has mass density function $\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{1}{9}xy,$ where $x$ and $y$ are measured in meters. The moment of inertia of the plate about a line $l$ perpendicular to the plate is $\\frac{13}{4}\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}.$\nGiven that the center of mass of the plate is at $(2,2),$ find the distance of the line $l$ from the center of mass." :question/worked-solution "For a plane lamina of mass $M$ and a line ${l}_{M}$ perpendicular to the lamina passing through its center of mass, the parallel axis theorem states that the moment of inertia of the lamina about a line $l$ parallel to ${l}_{M}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{l}={I}_{M}+{d}^{2}M,\n$$\n\nwhere $d$ is the distance between the lines, and ${I}_{M}$ and ${I}_{l}$ are the moments of inertia about the lines ${l}_{M}$ and $l,$ respectively.\n\nFirst, we find the mass of the plate as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{1}{9}xy\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}{\\int }_{0}^{3}x\\,\\text{d}x{\\int }_{0}^{3}y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}{|}_{0}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}{|}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\cdot \\frac{9}{2}\\cdot \\frac{9}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{4}\\,\\text{kg}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, the moment of inertia about the perpendicular line through the center of mass $(2,2)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{M} & ={∬}_{D}((x-\\overset{―}{x})^{2}+(y-\\overset{―}{y})^{2})\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}((x-2)^{2}+(y-2)^{2})\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{3}((x-2)^{2}+(y-2)^{2})\\cdot \\frac{1}{9}xy\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{3}x(x-2)^{2}y+x{y}^{3}-4x{y}^{2}+4xy\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{[\\frac{1}{2}x(x-2)^{2}{y}^{2}+\\frac{1}{4}x{y}^{4}-\\frac{4}{3}x{y}^{3}+2x{y}^{2}]}_{y=0}^{y=3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}+\\frac{9}{4}x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{8}{x}^{4}-\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3}+\\frac{9}{8}{x}^{2}]}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{81}{8}-18+\\frac{81}{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{4}\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the parallel axis theorem and solving for $d,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & ={I}_{M}+{d}^{2}M \\\\ \\frac{13}{4} & =\\frac{9}{4}+\\frac{9}{4}{d}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{9}{4}{d}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {d}^{2} & =\\frac{4}{9} \\\\ d & =\\frac{2}{3}\\,\\text{m}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "04477c12-4f2b-5395-8bf0-f85ecfa83b11" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500416}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500403 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500335 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "004ce097-91ab-590a-815b-3c51708ab242" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\,\\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499656} {:answer/id #uuid "49e665fd-5611-52a9-b2f7-a3eb3516948a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499795} {:answer/id #uuid "4a855d21-037d-5cfb-851f-7bb85c0b5055" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499796} {:answer/id #uuid "62c3f6a0-9789-54d2-8175-2efe5d82c60c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\,\\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499848} {:answer/id #uuid "a8022c0f-3c93-5f52-9405-a9fafc089d9c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}\\,\\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499980}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499656} :answer-field/id #uuid "a1c71d8a-386f-50b9-abbf-82d82b82978a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500245}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "a2307504-4cd4-513c-adab-5cca7db9b497" :question/math-academy-id "q-164134" :question/problem "A circular plate of mass $4\\,\\text{kg}$ with uniform mass density occupies the region of a disk $D$ of radius $\\sqrt{10}\\,\\text{m}$ and is centered at the origin.\nGiven that the moment of inertia of the plate about a line $l$ perpendicular to the plate is $56\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2},$ find the distance of the line $l$ from the center of mass of $D.$" :question/worked-solution "For a plane lamina of mass $M$ and a line ${l}_{M}$ perpendicular to the lamina passing through its center of mass, the parallel axis theorem states that the moment of inertia of the lamina about a line $l$ parallel to ${l}_{M}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{l}={I}_{M}+{d}^{2}M,\n$$\n\nwhere $d$ is the distance between the lines, and ${I}_{M}$ and ${I}_{l}$ are the moments of inertia about the lines ${l}_{M}$ and $l,$ respectively.\n\nFirst, notice that the region $D$ can be expressed in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nD=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le \\sqrt{10},\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nThe area of the circular plate $D$ is $A=\\pi \\cdot (\\sqrt{10})^{2}=10\\pi \\,{\\text{m}}^{2}.$\nSince the plate has a uniform density, the mass density function is given by\n\n$$\n\\lambda (r,\\theta )=\\frac{M}{A}=\\frac{4}{10\\pi }=\\frac{2}{5\\pi }\\,\\text{kg}/{\\text{m}}^{2},\n$$\n\nand its center of mass is its centroid $(0,0).$\n\nThen, the moment of inertia about the perpendicular line through the center of mass is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{M} & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{10}}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}{r}^{4}\\,{|}_{0}^{\\sqrt{10}}\\cdot \\theta \\,{|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5\\pi }\\cdot 25\\cdot 2\\pi  \\\\  & =20\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the parallel axis theorem and solving for $d,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & ={I}_{M}+{d}^{2}M \\\\ 56 & =20+4{d}^{2} \\\\ 4{d}^{2} & =36 \\\\ {d}^{2} & =9 \\\\ d & =3\\,\\text{m}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "4c0fd999-e432-5b94-beeb-049c81838f59"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500404 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129693 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5cf8414c-f8bd-5f0a-bb90-211754b3e2c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499836} {:answer/id #uuid "c88a091f-5193-58f8-a3ff-ce35311ba8f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi }{5}\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500063} {:answer/id #uuid "cfb59299-a1c0-5993-8c4a-30e7743496fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{10\\pi }{3}\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500080} {:answer/id #uuid "e625d291-c141-51ba-a082-9a6c4ff3253d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{16\\pi }{15}\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500135} {:answer/id #uuid "eb096d66-a9e1-570b-83d9-620ece8e6bb9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{8\\pi }{15}\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500140}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500135} :answer-field/id #uuid "0f3f5561-87af-512a-8ec3-695976a99517" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127639}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "424a10e5-7557-50d9-8260-a7eb80c40d48" :question/math-academy-id "q-38698" :question/problem "Calculate the moment of inertia of a circular plate that occupies the region of a disk $D$ of radius $1\\,\\text{m}$ with mass density $\\lambda (x,y)=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}},$ measured in $\\text{kg}/{\\text{m}}^{2},$ about a line perpendicular to the disk passing through a point on the circumference." :question/worked-solution "For a plane lamina of mass $M$ and a line ${l}_{M}$ perpendicular to the lamina passing through its center of mass, the parallel axis theorem states that the moment of inertia of the lamina about a line $l$ parallel to ${l}_{M}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{l}={I}_{M}+{d}^{2}M,\n$$\n\nwhere $d$ is the distance between the lines, and ${I}_{M}$ and ${I}_{l}$ are the moments of inertia about the lines ${l}_{M}$ and $l,$ respectively.\n\nFirst, notice that the region $D$ can be expressed in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nD=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nSince the mass density is symmetric in $x$ and $y,$ its center of mass is at $(0,0).$ The mass density function, in polar coordinates, is given by $\\lambda (r,\\theta )=\\sqrt{{r}^{2}}=r.$\n\nNext, we find the mass of the plate as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}r\\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{r}^{3}{|}_{0}^{1}\\cdot \\theta {|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\,\\text{kg}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, the moment of inertia about the perpendicular line through the center of mass is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{M} & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}r\\cdot {r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{r}^{4}\\,\\text{d}r\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}{r}^{5}{|}_{0}^{1}\\cdot \\theta {|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pi }{5}\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the parallel axis theorem, the moment of inertia about a line passing through a point on the circumference is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & ={I}_{M}+{d}^{2}M \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pi }{5}+{1}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{2\\pi }{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{6\\pi }{15}+\\frac{10\\pi }{15} \\\\  & =\\frac{16\\pi }{15}\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "4c58d4a3-e259-5a64-bfb1-3db86ff23f95" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500424}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500416 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500372 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9200e8ce-dffd-5f07-bf5d-9e83d7aa731d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "35\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499938} {:answer/id #uuid "aa9025ee-0539-5c8a-a017-7ac11e31f0d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "21\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499990} {:answer/id #uuid "b7f2a651-b2b4-50f6-b49c-4ff309a40cf2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "33\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500029} {:answer/id #uuid "e119a92f-e3a9-58ff-82e4-e4dd2ae6f760" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "18\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500123} {:answer/id #uuid "f4049635-7863-58c6-9d0b-18fbfbee2f14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "27\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500156}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500029} :answer-field/id #uuid "e3ca3a19-dcd8-503a-aaa3-09d81f94236f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500272}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "ea1b0433-500d-513e-b462-b96776148bf3" :question/math-academy-id "q-163813" :question/problem "A circular plate of mass $6\\,\\text{kg}$ and uniform mass density occupies the region of a disk $D$ of radius $3\\,\\text{m}$ centered at the origin.\nFind the moment of inertia of the plate about a line perpendicular to $D$ at a distance of $1\\,\\text{m}$ from a parallel line passing through its center of mass." :question/worked-solution "For a plane lamina of mass $M$ and a line ${l}_{M}$ perpendicular to the lamina passing through its center of mass, the parallel axis theorem states that the moment of inertia of the lamina about a line $l$ parallel to ${l}_{M}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{l}={I}_{M}+{d}^{2}M,\n$$\n\nwhere $d$ is the distance between the lines, and ${I}_{M}$ and ${I}_{l}$ are the moments of inertia about the lines ${l}_{M}$ and $l,$ respectively.\n\nFirst, notice that the region $D$ can be expressed in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nD=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 3,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nThe area of the circular plate $D$ is $A=\\pi \\cdot {3}^{2}=9\\pi \\,{\\text{m}}^{2}.$\nSince the plate has a uniform density, the mass density function is given by\n\n$$\n\\lambda (r,\\theta )=\\frac{M}{A}=\\frac{6}{9\\pi }=\\frac{2}{3\\pi }\\,\\text{kg}/{\\text{m}}^{2},\n$$\n\nand its center of mass is its centroid $(0,0).$\n\nThen, the moment of inertia about the perpendicular line through the center of mass is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{M} & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}{r}^{4}{|}_{0}^{3}\\cdot \\theta {|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{81}{4}\\cdot 2\\pi  \\\\  & =27\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the parallel axis theorem, the moment of inertia about a line $1\\,\\text{m}$ from the center of mass is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & ={I}_{M}+{d}^{2}M \\\\  & =27+{1}^{2}\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =27+6 \\\\  & =33\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "77423ec6-ca1b-55bf-9ea0-99d7b8c830db" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500403}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500424 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129548 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09074932-9828-54d0-9c71-0aa487eef82f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "56\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499673} {:answer/id #uuid "127fd677-7aeb-5f9c-8e85-204c2ec5b441" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "58\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499696} {:answer/id #uuid "4274eb44-f29d-5623-a87b-fb74baa6d6ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "52\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499780} {:answer/id #uuid "5b88ea4b-a61f-5187-8ed6-13ea9ed676f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "64\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499832} {:answer/id #uuid "e902174a-40ab-5cda-8e58-ebf0d676bfb1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "48\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500137}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499673} :answer-field/id #uuid "d60f4acf-292f-55a9-a007-bfa66a5bd1ea" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126785}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "5f04d615-f6b4-5013-9889-65ecf75e2ffc" :question/math-academy-id "q-163173" :question/problem "A rectangular plate of mass $12\\,\\text{kg}$ and uniform mass density occupies the region\n$D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 2\\},$\nwhere $x$ and $y$ are measured in meters.\nFind the moment of inertia of the plate about a line perpendicular to $D$ at a distance of $2\\,\\text{m}$ from a parallel line passing through its center of mass." :question/worked-solution "For a plane lamina of mass $M$ and a line ${l}_{M}$ perpendicular to the lamina passing through its center of mass, the parallel axis theorem states that the moment of inertia of the lamina about a line $l$ parallel to ${l}_{M}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{l}={I}_{M}+{d}^{2}M,\n$$\n\nwhere $d$ is the distance between the lines, and ${I}_{M}$ and ${I}_{l}$ are the moments of inertia about the lines ${l}_{M}$ and $l,$ respectively.\n\nThe area of the rectangular plate $D$ is $A=2\\cdot 2=4\\,{\\text{m}}^{2}.$\nSince the plate has a uniform density, the mass density function is given by\n\n$$\n\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{M}{A}=\\frac{12}{4}=3\\,\\text{kg}/{\\text{m}}^{2},\n$$\n\nand its center of mass is its centroid:\n\n$$\n(\\overset{¯}{x},\\overset{¯}{y})=(\\frac{0+2}{2},\\frac{0+2}{2})=(1,1)\n$$\n\nThen, the moment of inertia about the perpendicular line through the center of mass is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{M} & ={∬}_{D}((x-\\overset{¯}{x})^{2}+(y-\\overset{¯}{y})^{2})\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}3(x-1)^{2}+3(y-1)^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{[3(x-1)^{2}y+(y-1)^{3}]}_{y=0}^{y=2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}6(x-1)^{2}+2\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[2(x-1)^{3}+2x]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =2+4-(-2) \\\\  & =8\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the parallel axis theorem, the moment of inertia about a line $2\\,\\text{m}$ from the center of mass is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & ={I}_{M}+{d}^{2}M \\\\  & =8+{2}^{2}\\cdot 12 \\\\  & =8+48 \\\\  & =56\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "9445c9d9-c20e-5877-b779-cef068cded52" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500385}}] :activity/title "The Parallel Axis Theorem" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499652}]}
